/**

@page page_peano_amr Adaptive mesh refinement

\tableofcontents

Peano expresses dynamic adaptivity as cell requests and realises structural
changes through ordinary observer sweeps. A request names one cell whose
neighbourhood should refine or whose parent may erase. Refinement wins over
erasure wherever the requests conflict, and each drain can expose one
additional level on which a criterion can make a new decision.

# Grid control events

peano4::grid::GridControlEvent identifies the cell that raises a request by
three fields:

- `refinementControl` selects `Refine` or `Erase`.
- `level` is the cell level counted from the root.
- `latticeIndex` is the cell position on that level's lattice, which holds
  peano4::sfc::ActiveCurve::SubdivisionFactor cells per parent cell along each axis.

peano4::grid::GridControlEvent::forCell() converts a cell centre and width into
this exact lattice identity. Events are trivially copyable and can therefore
be exchanged between ranks as byte records. Their sequence order has no
precedence.

# Refinement-plan evaluation

peano4::topology::Tree::updateRefinementPlan() applies the request set through
integer lattice operations.

A refine request covers the named cell and one neighbouring cell in every
direction on the request level. On each coarser level, it covers the cells
containing the two ends of that band. Existing cells in these regions flag
their unrefined corner vertices. Levels finer than the requesting cell do not
participate.

An erase request names the parent cell whose children may disappear. A flagged
vertex is removed only when every existing adjacent cell asks to erase and no
refine request protects that region. Finer flagged vertices veto removal of a
coarse flag that they still require. All removals are evaluated against one
snapshot, so request order does not affect the result.

The resulting flag sets are sorted by lattice key. Every rank that receives
the same request set therefore derives the same refinement and ownership
plans. These plans determine consistent rank-local tree views, boundary pack
orders, and pool migrations without another structural exchange.

# Sweep lifecycle

The active observer returns its requests through `getGridControlEvents()`.
The topology manager compares a canonical request set with the last settled set
and skips repeated evaluations that leave the plan unchanged.

During grid construction, solver data is not persistent. A changed plan
rebuilds the structure directly, resizes the pools, and runs a creational
sweep. Further requests can extend the plan on subsequent construction
sweeps.

After grid initialisation, pool data must survive. A changed plan follows this
sequence:

1. Before traversal, the topology manager prepares the target plan, rank
   ownership, transition classes, and pool-slot mappings.
2. A stable single-owner delta appends to or retains the standing structure;
   other changes migrate the pools to the required union view.
3. The traversal emits create and destroy events and presents the standing and
   target refinement states to the generated storage guards.
4. An in-place transition prunes any erased region after the sweep. A rebuilding
   transition adopts its target view and transfers repartitioned pool slots.

The phase driver continues sweeping until the global grid reports the required
stationary sweeps.

# Producing requests

An action set normally accumulates requests while it visits cells and commits
them from `endTraversal()`. Observer clones keep private containers and merge
their results at the rank boundary of the step.

The application converts solver decisions into cell events. Its refinement
service sorts, deduplicates, and exchanges committed events so every rank
consumes the same vector before Peano evaluates them.

Erase decisions require two levels of agreement. Application-side control
promotes a leaf request to its parent only after all children and participating
criteria vote to erase. The spacetree then removes a parent flag only when the
complete adjacent cell ring agrees.

# Parallel behaviour

The refinement and ownership plans are replicated, while each rank materialises
only its spine, owned regions, and neighbour ring. Each rank observes requests
raised by its local cells, so a collective event exchange is required for a
distributed run. Local face and vertex propagation keeps boundary data
consistent, but it does not replace this global request set.

Repartitioning is part of the regrid transition. The target plan supplies leaf
weights for the deterministic rank partition, and the migration transfers
cell, face, and vertex pools whose ownership changes.

# Performance considerations

Plan evaluation scales with the request count and performs lattice lookups
without scanning the rank-local entity arrays. Regrid cost depends on the delta:
An in-place path updates affected topology, while a fallback builds the required
views and migrates their pools.

A criterion can request only a cell that exists. A feature that needs several
additional levels therefore produces another request after each newly exposed
level and requires several AMR drains. Stable criteria should stop emitting
requests once the standing plan satisfies them, and erase criteria should
avoid alternating with protective refine requests.

*/
